Polymyalgia Rheumatica (Mild Atypical)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Mild morning stiffness, borderline ESR/CRP elevation, highly responsive to very low-dose steroids

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe proximal muscle pain and immobility requiring exclusion of acute cervical myelopathy or occult fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, immobilizing shoulder and pelvic girdle pain in an older adult. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to differentiate severe inflammatory stiffness from an acute spinal cord compromise, severe acute radiculopathy, or an occult proximal fracture prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I went to get out of bed tonight and my shoulders and hips were locked solid. The pain is a deep, paralyzing ache, and I cannot lift my arms above my chest.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a recent history of low-grade shoulder/hip girdle ache in older adults, previously attributed to osteoarthritis. Tonight, following a period of prolonged rest in a cold room, the patient experienced an acute, severe exacerbation of proximal stiffness and pain. The patient became completely unable to dress or mobilize independently. Denies recent falls, trauma, localized joint swelling, headache, jaw claudication, or visual changes.
Functional Status: Currently unable to stand from a seated position without significant upper limb assistance; upper limb abduction limited to 45 degrees.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, acute pain severely restricting independent mobilization and self-care tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable, moving extremely slowly to minimize proximal muscle engagement.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals profound stiffness and deep tenderness over the bilateral deltoid, trapezius, and gluteal muscle groups. Mild morning stiffness, borderline ESR/CRP elevation, highly responsive to very low-dose steroids (noted from recent outpatient labs). No focal neurological deficits; distal muscle power (grip strength, ankle dorsiflexion) is entirely normal (5/5). Peripheral joints (wrists, knees) lack effusions or warmth. Cranial nerve exam and temporal artery palpation are strictly normal.
Environmental Observations: House temperature cold; heating initiated.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Using paracetamol with nil effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Polymyalgia Rheumatica (Mild Atypical) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Occult Proximal Fracture / Trauma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical presentation without a mechanism of injury, and lack of acute localized bony point tenderness.

Giant Cell Arteritis (Systemic Phase) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of visual disturbance, temporal headache, scalp tenderness, or jaw claudication.

Cervical Myelopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact distal neurological function, negative upper motor neuron signs, and pain purely muscular rather than radicular or neuropathic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute functional decline)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, immobilizing proximal muscle stiffness, mimicking a severe neurological or orthopedic crisis. Triage correctly mandated urgent physical evaluation to exclude cord compression or occult fracture. Bedside assessment definitively established intact neurological function and isolated the pathology to a severe inflammatory spasm of the proximal muscle girdles, confirming an acute PMR flare. The prompt administration of targeted analgesia and confirmation of safety was critical to prevent an unnecessary, highly painful ambulance transfer, completely satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs for acute relief pending steroid initiation. Guided patient through slow, progressive active range-of-motion exercises.
Positive Behavioral Change: Set your bedroom thermostat slightly higher and take a hot shower immediately upon waking. By physically warming the large muscle groups before moving, you will rapidly reduce the intense stiffness, allowing you to dress and mobilize without the paralyzing pain you experienced tonight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to commence low-dose oral corticosteroids (e.g., prednisolone 15mg).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop any sudden blurring or loss of vision, a severe headache on the side of your head, or if your scalp becomes intensely painful when brushing your hair.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending trial of oral steroids; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: PMR causes severe inflammatory stiffness, not structural joint damage. It is exquisitely sensitive to low-dose steroids, which typically resolve symptoms dramatically within 24 to 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the pain migrates to your small joints (hands/feet) or if you develop a true fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is causing generalized inflammation in the large muscle groups of your shoulders and hips. The nerves and bones are perfectly safe. The impending steroid medication will turn this inflammation off rapidly.
Actionable Steps: Avoid prolonged periods of total rest during the day. Use heat packs on your shoulders tonight. You must see your GP tomorrow for a steroid prescription.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Polymyalgia Rheumatica (inflammatory muscle stiffness). No fractures or nerve damage. Safe to rest. Must see GP tomorrow morning for steroid prescription.

SMS EDUCATION

Use heat packs and keep your environment warm to reduce stiffness. Go straight to ED if you develop any visual changes, severe headache, or jaw pain.
